    "type": "question",
    "label": "2",
    "prompt": "Prove that $5<\\sqrt6+\\sqrt7$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Assuming $5\\ge\\sqrt6+\\sqrt7$ and squaring twice gives $36\\ge42$, a contradiction.",
      "worked": "Suppose the statement is false. All quantities being squared below are non-negative.\n$$\\begin{align*}5&\\ge\\sqrt6+\\sqrt7\\\\5^2&\\ge(\\sqrt6+\\sqrt7)^2\\\\25&\\ge6+2\\sqrt{42}+7\\\\12&\\ge2\\sqrt{42}\\\\6&\\ge\\sqrt{42}\\\\6^2&\\ge42\\\\36&\\ge42.\\end{align*}$$\nThis is impossible. Therefore, $5<\\sqrt6+\\sqrt7$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 84
  },
